The ambiguity comes from `Application.Domain.setMock` having overloads (`setMock(SObjectType, fflib_SObjectDomain)` and `setMock(fflib_SObjectDomain)`), so passing an interface-typed mock is ambiguous. I need to cast the mock to `fflib_SObjectDomain` explicitly (the mock object actually implements both the interface and extends the base class under ApexMocks dynamic stubbing)... Actually with fflib_ApexMocks, `mocks.mock(IOpportunities.class)` only produces a proxy implementing `IOpportunities`, not extending `fflib_SObjectDomain`. The resolution is to use the single-arg `setMock(fflib_SObjectDomain)` by casting through `fflib_SObjectDomain`, but since it's just an interface mock, we should instead call `Application.Domain.setMock(Opportunity.SObjectType, domainMock)` to pick the unambiguous overload explicitly.

